---
import PluginDocsLayout from '../../layouts/PluginDocsLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';

const testShape = `// tests/example_plugin_test.cpp, the reference test for the hello plugin.
TEST(example_hello_plugin_contributes_and_unwinds_a_tool) {
    ScratchConfig scratch("hello");       // redirects XDG_CONFIG_HOME
    Fixture f;                            // ToolRegistry + Config + Workspace
    PluginRuntime runtime(f.tools, f.cfg, f.ws);
    runtime.add(std::make_shared<examples::HelloPlugin>());

    ASSERT_FALSE((bool)f.tools.find("greet"));   // registered, not started

    runtime.start();
    ASSERT_TRUE(runtime.status("hello").enabled);
    auto tool = f.tools.find("greet");
    ASSERT_TRUE((bool)tool);
    ASSERT_EQ(tool->execute(json{{"name", "amber"}}).output,
              std::string("Hello, amber!"));

    ASSERT_TRUE(runtime.set_state("hello", false));   // disable
    ASSERT_FALSE((bool)f.tools.find("greet"));        // ledger unwound
}`;

const gates = `make clean && make && make test && make lint && make analyze && make check`;

const checklist = `- [ ] Plugin depends only on the public plugin API, no tui/ includes.
- [ ] Every contribution goes through a capability; nothing registered
      in initialize outside the ledger.
- [ ] Render callables are pure; no I/O, no blocking, no mutation.
- [ ] No blocking in event handlers.
- [ ] Tests: contribution, removal, and one hermetic end-to-end path.
- [ ] The availability table is updated if a capability's status changed.
- [ ] Zero new clang-tidy/cppcheck findings; class <= 200 lines,
      method <= 10 lines.`;
---

<PluginDocsLayout
  title="Testing & review"
  description="How to test an amber plugin hermetically and the checklist a plugin PR is reviewed against."
  current="testing"
>
  <h1>Testing &amp; review</h1>

  <p>
    Every contribution lands with a failing test first (red), then the
    implementation (green), then a PR that keeps the gates green on both
    <code>g++</code> and <code>clang++</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The four test layers</h2>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li><strong>Unit</strong>: capabilities against a fake <code>PluginServices</code>; assert what you contribute and that removal is total.</li>
    <li><strong>Hermetic integration</strong>: register your plugin in a <code>PluginRuntime</code>, drive a turn with the fake LLM client (<code>tests/fake_llm.h</code>), assert observable effects. <strong>No network, ever.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Dialect tests</strong> (providers), pure body/parse/stream fixtures, mirroring <code>tests/dialect_gemini_test.cpp</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Regression</strong>: <code>make test</code>; your contributions must not leak into other tests. The ledger test exists to catch exactly this.</li>
  </ul>
  <Diagram
    name="testLayers"
  />

  <h2>Redirection and hermeticity</h2>
  <p>
    Plugin state is persisted under <code>~/.config/amber/plugins/&lt;id&gt;/</code>.
    A test that activates a plugin must redirect <code>XDG_CONFIG_HOME</code> to
    a scratch directory so it never touches the developer's real config. The
    reference test does this with a small RAII guard.
  </p>

  <h2>The reference test</h2>
  <div class="terminal"><pre>{testShape}</pre></div>
  <p>
    This is the shape to copy: register, assert nothing is installed, start,
    assert the contribution is live, disable, assert it is gone. It is compiled
    from <code>examples/plugin_hello/</code> by <code>make test</code>, so it
    cannot rot.
  </p>

  <h2>Doc-as-test</h2>
  <p>
    <code>tests/plugin_docs_test.cpp</code> asserts that every
    <code>CapabilityKind</code>, every event payload struct, and every bundled
    plugin id is documented in the developer guide. Add a capability or an event
    without updating the guide and the suite fails, documentation drift is
    caught in CI, not in review.
  </p>

  <h2>The gates</h2>
  <div class="terminal"><pre>{gates}</pre></div>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li><code>make test</code>: the unit suite (hermetic).</li>
    <li><code>make check</code>: build-hygiene invariants.</li>
    <li><code>make lint</code>: clang-tidy; <code>make analyze</code>: cppcheck. Both must be clean.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Review checklist</h2>
  <div class="terminal"><pre>{checklist}</pre></div>

  <h2>Prove the seam held</h2>
  <p>
    A provider or tool plugin's diff must not touch
    <code>lib/http_transport.cpp</code>, the agent loop, or the TUI. If it does,
    the framework, not your plugin, is missing an extension point. Say so in
    the PR instead of working around it.
  </p>
</PluginDocsLayout>
